## 9. Success Criteria

The project is succeeding if the following become true.

### Minimal success

* the simulator reliably produces traces with known recoverable structure,
* oracle UAD scores validate the intended ε-blankets,
* the DNN learns non-degenerate slots,
* latent slots improve simple downstream structure discovery over raw variables in at least some low-sample regimes.

### Strong success

* latent-UAD requires fewer samples than raw-UAD across a broad region of the simulator grid,
* learned slots separate global confounds from agent-relevant variables,
* discovered latent clusters map back to raw variables matching ground truth,
* optional agency regularization improves recovery under confounds/leakage without overfitting.

### Failure but still informative

The approach is failing usefully if:

* predictive latents consistently delete action/control variables,
* regularizers create false agency,
* raw-space falsification rejects most latent discoveries,
* latent-UAD only works when oracle structure is almost obvious.

These failures would indicate that the representation objective needs stronger causal/interventional constraints.

